{{- /*
  jira/resolve-query — canonical JQL-resolution ladder: a per-run `Query`
  argument override (raw JQL or a JIRA URL: saved filter / RapidBoard /
  browse link) → the conversation's saved "Jira Tasks" user data → an
  interactive ask-and-persist fallback (with the mitto_workspace_update
  schema-registration retry) / silent-mode notify-and-stop. Extracted per
  mitto-w8jp.2 from sync-tasks Step 1, which is also the canonical source for
  the "never guess a JQL" invariant.

  Callers pass a dict:
    "Ctx"  — the full render context (`.`), forwarded so `.Args.Query`,
             `UserData`, and `.Session.ID` resolve against the real session.
             Required.
    "Next" — the short imperative sentence naming the caller's own next step
             once a JQL string is in hand (e.g. "continue to Step 2" or
             "continue to Step 3 — Search for an existing ticket"). Required;
             every ladder exit substitutes this in place of a hardcoded
             "continue to Step 2".

  Usage:

      {{ template "jira/shared/resolve-query" (dict "Ctx" . "Next" "continue to Step 2") }}
*/ -}}
{{- $ctx := .Ctx -}}
{{- $next := .Next -}}
{{ if $ctx.Args.Query -}}
A per-run **Query override** was supplied for this invocation:

> `{{ $ctx.Args.Query }}`

Resolve it to a JQL string, then use that as the JQL and {{ $next }} (this value is
used for **this run only** — do **not** persist it to the conversation's user
data). Detect the shape and act accordingly:

- **Raw JQL** — the value does **not** start with `http://` or `https://`. Use it
  verbatim as the JQL query and {{ $next }}.
- **JIRA URL** — the value starts with `http://` or `https://`. Parse the URL and
  convert it into an equivalent JQL string using the query string parameters and
  (when needed) the JIRA MCP tools / `jira` CLI:
  - `.../issues/?filter=<ID>` or `.../issues/?jql=<ENCODED_JQL>` — use `jql`
    directly (URL-decoded) or resolve the saved filter (`jira_get_filter` if
    available; otherwise `jira filter view <ID>`) and use its JQL.
  - `.../secure/RapidBoard.jspa?rapidView=<BOARD_ID>[&quickFilter=<QF_ID>]` — the
    board's underlying filter is its JQL. Fetch the board (`jira_get_agile_board`
    / `jira_get_board_filter` if available; otherwise `jira board list --raw`
    + `jira filter view`) and use the board filter's JQL. If a `quickFilter` id
    is present, AND its clause into the board JQL as well (quick-filter clauses
    are stored on the board configuration).
  - `.../browse/<KEY>` — single-ticket link; use `issuekey = <KEY>` as the JQL.
  - Any `selectedIssue=<KEY>` query parameter (e.g. from a RapidBoard link) is a
    UI cursor and must be **ignored** — do not narrow the query to a single issue
    unless the URL is a `.../browse/<KEY>` link.
  - If the URL cannot be resolved (unknown host/shape, missing filter permission,
    MCP tool unavailable), fall back to the saved-query path below in interactive
    mode, or notify-and-stop in silent mode. Never guess.

Once you have a JQL string, {{ $next }}.
{{- else if UserData "Jira Tasks" -}}
The saved JQL query for this conversation is:

> `{{ UserData "Jira Tasks" }}`

Use this as the JQL query and {{ $next }}.
{{- else }}

No "Jira Tasks" query is saved for this conversation.

- **Silent mode** (scheduled loop run): the query cannot be requested unattended. Send one `mitto_ui_notify` explaining the project has no "Jira Tasks" query configured, then **stop**.

- **Interactive mode**: ask the user for the JQL query with `mitto_ui_form` (a single text field for the query, e.g. `project = ABC AND statusCategory != Done`). Once you have a non-empty value, persist it to the conversation's user data so future runs (including scheduled ones) reuse it:

  ```
  mitto_conversation_update(self_id: "{{ $ctx.Session.ID }}",
    conversation_id: "{{ $ctx.Session.ID }}",
    user_data: [{"name": "Jira Tasks", "value": "<the JQL the user provided>"}])
  ```

  **Ensure the schema allows the attribute first.** User data is validated against the workspace's user-data **schema** (in `.mittorc`). If that schema does not define a **"Jira Tasks"** field, the `mitto_conversation_update` above will be **rejected** (e.g. `unknown attribute "Jira Tasks": not defined in schema`, or `no user data schema defined for this workspace`). To make the save succeed, add the field to the schema with `mitto_workspace_update` (this edits the workspace `.mittorc`), then retry the conversation update:

  ```
  mitto_workspace_update(self_id: "{{ $ctx.Session.ID }}",
    user_data_schema: [{"name": "Jira Tasks",
                        "description": "JQL query selecting the JIRA tickets to mirror into beads",
                        "type": "string"}],
    user_data_schema_merge: true)
  ```

  `user_data_schema_merge: true` (the default) merges this field into the existing schema by name, so it **adds** "Jira Tasks" without disturbing other defined fields. Practically: attempt the `mitto_conversation_update` first; if it fails with a schema/unknown-attribute error, call `mitto_workspace_update` to register the field, then repeat the `mitto_conversation_update`. (Proactively calling `mitto_workspace_update` before the first save is also fine, since the merge is idempotent.)

  Then use that JQL query for this run and {{ $next }}.
{{- end }}
